WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.500
Okay, we finally got that working so I'm going
to turn it over to Kate now. Thanks Peter.

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:13.030
Alright, I'm going to jump right in. I'm going
over to PubMed and if you have another browser

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:18.880
open you can follow right along.
To search for a gene in PubMed, enter the

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:26.779
symbol or name into the search box. We'll
search: ANK1

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:31.939
For many genes, the PubMed Gene Sensor will
kick in, and a box will appear at the top

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:36.500
of your results, linking you to the relevant
records in the Gene Database.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.940
You also see a link at the top to retrieve
PubMed records for articles about this gene.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.370
This link is created by GeneRIFs (or Reference
into Function), which I'll explain in a

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.879
minute.
The next main link takes you to record in

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:55.110
the gene database for the human gene. Because
we�re NIH, we focus on information about

00:00:55.110 --> 00:01:02.670
humans, so the Gene database primarily represents
genes in humans and some model organism species.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:07.090
Follow the link to the specific species of
interest. In this example, we'll stick with

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:11.040
humans and I'll use the main link.
Linking from the Gene Database

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.130
Consider the Gene Database record your gene's
home page. This is where we'd like you to

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:23.590
start any exploration of a gene in the NCBI
databases, including explorations of the literature.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:33.950
In the right column of the gene record, under
Related information, you'll find links back

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:41.430
to PubMed. Use these links to find literature
about a gene. Hovering your mouse over the

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:47.540
link will open an explanation for each. I'll
highlight two:

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:52.920
The link labeled PubMed will retrieve articles
selected by NLM curators and generated from

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.070
external databases. And a full explanation
is there.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:03.030
The link labeled GeneRIFs (for Reference into
Function) retrieves articles that focus on

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:09.259
the function of a gene. These articles are
selected by NLM Staff, but also volunteer

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:14.500
collaborators, maybe you!
When we identify articles that inform our

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:19.620
understanding of gene function, we extract
relevant excerpts from these articles discussing

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:27.280
the function of the gene. We call these GeneRIFS
and we include them in the Gene database record,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.420
below.
You can help with this! You can submit your

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.310
own GeneRIF. If you have published or know
about an article that describes the function

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:42.920
of a gene and it is not yet included in this
GeneRIF section of a gene record, please participate.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.150
You can follow this link here, and I'll
put a link in the chat box to more information

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.209
and instructions on how to participate in
this crowd-sourcing project: http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene/about-generif

00:02:53.209 --> 00:03:01.209
So again going back to the Gene record, for
Ank1 in Homo sapiens, these links to Related

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:07.599
information,from the Gene Database to PubMed
will lead you to highly relevant literature

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.900
about the gene.
This is the main point I want to make today:

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:16.080
Using the Gene Database to link back to PubMed
will retrieve the most relevant current articles

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.920
referenced in PubMed about a gene.
If for some reason the Gene Sensor isn't activated

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:28.560
when you search PubMed, search the Gene Database
directly. I'll go back to PubMed to clarify

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.480
how to get here.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:44.959
Let's say we tried the search APOO (apolipoprotein-O)
in PubMed. No gene sensor. Some terms are

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:50.140
too ambiguous to trigger the gene sensor.
So I'll select the Gene Database from the

00:03:50.140 --> 00:04:00.019
database selection menu in the upper left
and search the gene name or symbol: APOO.

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:08.340
Here I can select the gene record for my species,
and from the full Gene database record I can

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:18.060
use those links to retrieve citations in PubMed.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.680
For a more a comprehensive search of the literature,
you will need to search PubMed directly. Articles

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.540
about a gene are indexed using the name of
the resultant protein, with the subheading,

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:32.820
genetics.
To go back to my first example, if I search

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:44.400
for the protein ANK1 and genetics and maybe
add human for greater specificity, I'll get

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:51.389
the PubMed results, and, just for fun, note
that I'm sorting by relevance. By default

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:57.630
your sort is by Most recent but you can sort
by relevance.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:02.020
I'd like to show you why I get pretty good
results for this search by looking at this

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:24.009
first record you'll see the indexing applied.
It is indexed with the terms, Ankyrins/genetics

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:32.440
and under Substances you can see ANK1 protein,
human. Understanding the indexing can improve

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.259
your searching.

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:37.810
If you'd like to search using gene names or
symbols that might appear in the title or

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:43.669
abstract, be aware that many genes have had
more than one name over time, so you may need

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:51.509
to search using synonyms. The Gene database
includes a wide range of synonyms.

00:05:51.509 --> 00:06:10.419
For example, if I search for tp53 in the Gene
Database, selecting that human record, I can

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:18.000
see in the summary section that it has several
synonyms, including a very common synonym,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.819
p53. So it's TP53 and P53 and these other
synonyms.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:32.000
Another useful reference to find synonyms
is genenames.org which happens to be linked

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.259
right here from this Gene record. So I'm going
to follow this link, to grab that URL to share

00:06:38.259 --> 00:06:39.550
with you in the chat box.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:46.490
Again, to find the decidedly relevant literature,
I would use the links from the tp53 record

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:52.470
in the Gene database. To retrieve all potentially
relevant literature, for example, to create

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:57.229
a saved search and be alerted to anything
new in PubMed with any of these synonyms,

00:06:57.229 --> 00:07:03.380
I could construct a search for all of these
synonyms using an OR statement. So my PubMed

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:27.289
search might be something like, tp53 OR P53
OR BCC7 OR LFS1 OR TRP53. And you can see

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:30.750
from the results that I am getting quite a
few. And

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:36.009
I should expect false hits from the more comprehensive
search.

00:07:36.009 --> 00:07:39.970
Part of the problem, which I alluded to earlier
when I was talking about the gene sensor,

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:46.090
is that many gene symbols are not uniquely
identifiable. For example, COX2 might be cyclooxygenase

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:51.270
2 or cytochrome-c oxidase, so not only has
it been used to describe different genes,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:56.599
it also has different meanings in the more
general medical vocabulary. This is why controlled

00:07:56.599 --> 00:08:01.599
vocabulary systems are so important, and why
searching the literature that has been described

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:06.150
by indexers is easier than searching the non-indexed
literature.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:11.330
What would make our databases function even
better is if scientists like you would use

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:28.099
gene IDs in your publications.
And I'm going to show you an example here.

00:08:28.099 --> 00:08:36.269
So take a look at this abstract. ANd you can
see that not only did they put the gene ID,

00:08:36.269 --> 00:08:42.589
the uniquely identifiable gene ID, in the
article, it's right in the abstract. So

00:08:42.589 --> 00:08:48.330
including gene ID's in your publications allows
us to much more easily link from your publications

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:53.860
to gene information, and vice versa.
I'll put a link into the Chat box for background

00:08:53.860 --> 00:09:05.610
and rationale on linking between articles
and gene databases: [http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/22019431]

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:11.279
To review the four points I've made today.
First, use the links from the Gene Database

00:09:11.279 --> 00:09:17.140
to search PubMed for relevant literature about
a gene. I'll go to the Gene database homepage

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:23.860
as a visual queue to remind you of that.
Secondly, our indexers describe genes by the

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:28.680
protein, so you can use the protein terms
and the word genetics for searching PubMed.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:33.810
Third, if you need to search comprehensively,
you'll need to construct a search for the

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.990
synonyms used to describe a gene and expect
to work through false hits.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:44.300
Fourth, help us help you! You can help improve
knowledge about genes by, using gene ids in

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:50.130
your publications, and submitting GeneRIFS
there's a link right here under Gene tools

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:55.410
on the Gene homepage, when you are aware of
literature describing the function of a gene.

